<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of the East</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nestorian Christian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of the East is an ancient Christian church centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, often called Nestorian. Its missionaries spread its faith across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> in 635, as recorded on the famous Nestorian Stele.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east.jpg" alt="Church of the East" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baomi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_epitaph_of_a_Nestorian_Christian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Church of the East, also called the East Syriac Church, the Persian Church or the Nestorian Church, arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, where Christian communities were forming as early as the 1st century. It traced its origins to Thaddeus of Edessa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a>. Sasanian rule from 266 brought significant communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>.</p><p>In 315 Papa bar Aggai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Seleucia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, imposed the primacy of his see and took the title of Catholicos. The Council of Seleucia-Ctesiphon of 410 organised the church as the national church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, and in 424 it declared itself independent of the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The church shared communion with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s churches until the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestorius">Nestorius</a> in 431. The Church of the East refused to condemn him and was therefore called Nestorian by the Roman church, a name that scholars have since called a misnomer. After 431 the Roman authorities suppressed Nestorianism, a reason for Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to favour it. After the Muslim conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in 644, the church became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a> minority under the Patriarch of the East, restricted but protected, and its missionaries were allowed to work elsewhere.</p><p>Missionaries reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians">Saint Thomas Christians</a> kept a long association with the church, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The Nestorian Stele, set up on 7 January 781 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, credits a mission under the Persian cleric Alopen in 635 with introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> the church reached its widest extent in the late 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s sons took wives from the Christian Kerait clan. Decline followed: there were 68 cities with resident <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in 1000, 24 in 1238 and only seven at the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1405.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the middle of the 16th century a series of schisms produced rival patriarchates, some of them uniting with the Catholic Church. The traditionalist branch became the Assyrian Church of the East, and the groups united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> became the Chaldean Catholic Church. In the later 20th century the traditionalist patriarchate itself divided into the Assyrian Church of the East and the Ancient Church of the East. The Syro-Malabar Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> also claims the heritage of the Church of the East.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church of the East” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 549 they asked the patriarch of the <b>Church of the East</b>, Mar Aba I, to consecrate a priest they had chosen as their bishop.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nestorian Christians</b>, Zoroastrians, Manichaeans and Buddhists maintained temples and monasteries in the capital, alongside communities of Muslim merchants, while the state employed Sogdian financiers, Turkic generals, Korean officials and scholars from Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Council of Chalcedon in 451: the Coptic, Ethiopian, Armenian and Syriac Orthodox churches rejected its definition, and the <b>Church of the East</b>, centred in Persia, carried its own tradition along the Silk Road to India and China, where a stele of 781 at Xi’an records its mission.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesopotamian religion had almost completely died out by about 400–500 CE, after its people had largely become Syriac Christians; Assyria became a centre of the <b>Church of the East</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Genghis Khan and several generations of his successors were Tengrist “shaman-kings”, open to other faiths: Hulegu, who ruled from Maragheh in Iran, had <b>Nestorian Christians</b> in his family.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Missionaries of the <b>Church of the East</b> carried Syriac to India, Central Asia and China, where a stone set up at Xi’an in 781 records the arrival of Christianity in Chinese and Syriac.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
